        <td>9:00 - 9:30</td>
        <td><strong>Keynote 1: </strong>
           Nesime Tatbul (Intel/MIT) <br> <i>Scalable Data Management and Analytics for Observability Applications</i>
                     <br/>
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#keynote1A')">Click to toggle abstract and bio.</a>
          <div id="keynote1A" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <h3>Abstract</h3>
            <p>
              Observability is the ability to understand and control the behavior of large-scale software systems using run-time telemetry. Managing observability data involves handling massive volumes of heterogeneous time series while operating under tight resource constraints. Troubleshooting the observed system requires efficient exploration of this data through interactive queries with time-oriented operations. Advances in AI have enabled more sophisticated data analysis techniques such as anomaly detection and explanation. This talk presents examples from our recent research in data management for modern observability in response to these challenges and opportunities.

              </p>
            <h3>Bio</h3>
            <p>
              <b>Nesime Tatbul </b> is a Senior Staff Research Scientist at Intel. For the last 10+ years, she has been based at MIT, overseeing Intel's university research programs on data systems and artificial intelligence. Previously, she received a Ph.D. from Brown University and held a faculty position at ETH Zurich. Her research contributions in data stream systems, time series analytics, and learned data management have been widely cited and recognized by awards. Nesime is an ACM Distinguished Member, an IEEE Senior Member, and a Trustee Emeritus of the VLDB Endowment.

          </div>
          </td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>9:30 - 9:45</td>
        <td>
                          
          <i>DiskJoin: Large-scale Vector Similarity Join with SSD</i><br />
          Yanqi Chen (University of Massachusetts Amherst)*; Xiao Yan (Institute for Math & AI, Wuhan, Wuhan University); Alexandra Meliou (University of Massachusetts Amherst); Eric Lo (The Chinese University of Hong Kong) <br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po12')">Click to Toggle Abstract </a> 
          <div id="po12" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
              Vector similarity join is a fundamental operation in modern data management and machine learning pipelines, enabling applications such as large-scale deduplication, outlier detection, and dataset curation. Prior work has explored distributed computation methods to scale similarity join to large data volumes but these methods require a cluster deployment, and efficiency suffers from expensive inter-machine communication. On the other hand, disk-based solutions are more cost-effective by using a single machine and storing the large dataset on high-performance external storage, such as NVMe SSDs, but in these methods the disk I/O time is a serious bottleneck. <br/>In this talk, we present DiskJoin, the first disk-based similarity join algorithm that can process billion-scale vector datasets efficiently on a single machine. DiskJoin improves disk I/O by tailoring the data access patterns to avoid repetitive accesses and read amplification. It also uses main memory as a dynamic cache and carefully manages cache eviction to improve cache hit rate and reduce disk retrieval time. For further acceleration, we adopt a probabilistic pruning technique that can effectively prune a large number of vector pairs from computation. Our evaluation on real-world, large-scale datasets shows that DiskJoin significantly outperforms alternatives, achieving speedups from 50x to 1000x.
          </div>
        </td>

        
      </tr>

            <tr>
        <td>9:45 - 10:00</td>
        <td>
                    <i>TurtleKV: A Dynamically Tunable Key/Value Store</i><br />
                    Anthony Astolfi (The MathWorks)*; 
Vidya Silai (The MathWorks); 
Darby Huye (Tufts University); 
Lan Liu (Tufts University); 
Raja Sambasivan (Tufts University); 
Johes Bater (Tufts University)<br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po46')">Click to Toggle Abstract</a> 
<br />
          <div id="po46" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
Key/Value Storage Engines, a crucial building block for modern databases and applications, are constrained by fundamental resource cost trade-offs that prevent simultaneous optimal tuning for all workloads. The basic, two-way trade-offs from which this constraint emerges are not equally advantageous as tuning knobs, however: altering the stored structure of data to target a tuning point (i.e., trading reads against writes) makes it expensive <br/>to change the tuning objective later. Memory-based trade-offs (read-memory, write-memory) offer lower retuning costs, but write-vs-memory, or WM tuning is poorly supported in current system designs. We present TurtleKV, a new key/value storage engine utilizing a novel external memory dictionary, the TurtleTree, as an alternative. TurtleKV, though its selection of primary data structure and multi-core optimized system design, enables dynamic, runtime tuning of read-memory and write-memory trade-offs to target optimal performance for changing workloads. In the Yahoo Cloud Serving Benchmark (YCSB) TurtleKV is able to match or exceed the performance of state-of-the-art storage engines SplinterDB, RocksDB, and WiredTiger.
              </div>
        </td>
        
      </tr>
          </td>
      </tr>



      <tr>
        <td>10:00 - 10:15</td>
        <td>
                

          <i>Meeting Latency SLOs on Cloud Data Warehouses</i><br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po20')">Click to Toggle Abstract</a> Markos Markakis (Massachusetts Institute of Technology)*; 
Ziniu Wu (Massachusetts Institute of Technology); 
Tim Kraska (Massachusetts Institute of Technology)
<br />
          <div id="po20" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
          As the cloud-based data warehousing market matures, low technical configuration requirements are emerging as an important selling point. Where customers once had to carefully provision VMs, today's "serverless" model abstracts away most of the hardware details. In the next phase of this evolution, vendors are examining cost/performance constraints as configuration inputs, moving away from explicit mentions of hardware at all. In one formulation, they aim to deliver the best performance under some dollar budget. In the formulation we focus on, one aims for acceptable latency as cheaply as possible, where "acceptable" is defined as queries meeting their latency Service-Level Objectives (SLOs). In this work, we present a framework for assembling and operating multi-compute-instance cloud-based data warehouses under a latency SLO. We discuss the opportunities afforded by recent ML advances in the field, and describe promising paths forward and their early results. This is ongoing work.
           </div>
