      <tr>
        <td>11:30 - 11:45</td>
        <td>
                    <i>Space Efficient Mapping Structures for Learned Indexes</i><br />
                    Anwesha Saha (Boston University)*; 
Aneesh Raman (Boston University); 
Ryan Marcus (University of Pennsylvania); 
Manos Athanassoulis (Boston University)<br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po15')"> Click to Toggle Abstract</a> 
          <div id="po15" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
            Learned indexes are strong competitors to classical indexes like B+Trees due to efficient query performance and low space utilization. They operate by replacing the internal nodes of the index with a hierarchy of machine learning models that capture the data distribution. However, to achieve high space savings in practical indexing structures, learned indexes require a sorted version of the underlying data. We therefore explore lightweight mapping structures that can accurately translate the sorted positions predicted by the learned index to their actual physical locations in the base data. We focus on structures that implement this sorted-to-physical mapping by storing the permutation and balancing the space–time tradeoffs involved.<br/>Our initial work explored wavelet tree-based representations of this permutation to reduce memory footprint, but the traditional structure incurred high cache misses due to binary splits at each level. We extended this to a multiway variant, but this added complexity and required higher memory due to metadata needed for efficient access. Because of these tradeoffs, this structure could not surpass state-of-the-art permutation storage.<br/><br/>To address these challenges, we introduce a new data structure, Constellation Maps, which models the permutation in 2-D space. A set of points is represented as (i, p[i]) implying that the i-th sorted element exists at position p[i] in the original data. We encode these points using a set of lines (slope and intercept) along with a small error (a few bits). When the data exhibits structure, very few lines can map all points (e.g., a sorted group requires a single line through the origin). Moreover, if most points map to a few lines, these hot lines can be efficiently cached, leading to extremely fast access.<br/><br/>However, finding the best set of lines is computationally difficult. Linear solvers exist but do not scale to large datasets, and different line-drawing algorithms model specific distributions best. We currently explore multiple methods: RANSAC (a consensus-based greedy algorithm), recursively fitted linear regression, a greedy line-sampling approach, and a grid-voting algorithm using the Hough transform. Across these methods, selecting sampling strategies, budgets, stopping criteria, and loss functions is nontrivial. While the problem resembles set cover, the interaction between error bounds, coverage skew, and access efficiency makes it substantially harder and requires adaptive, structure-aware builders.<br/><br/>Ultimately, we aim to make learned indexes practically deployable by making theoretically efficient indexing structures feasible through structure-aware designs that combine data structures with learning and algorithmic techniques while preserving learned indexes’ space–time advantages in real systems.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>
          </td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>11:45 - 12:00</td>
        <td>
                  <i>Augmenting Adaptive Filters with Ski Rental Policies</i><br />
                  Yuvaraj Chesetti (Northeastern University)*;
Prashant Pandey (Northeastern University)<br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po16')">Click to Toggle Abstract</a> 
          <div id="po16" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
           The talk will present ongoing researching work on applying online decision policies to adaptive filters. Adaptive filters are filters that correct false positives dynamically, solving traditional filters' key weakness: static representations that yield near-100\% error rates on skewed workloads. <br/> <br/>By updating their representation upon detecting false positives, adaptive filters bound error rates regardless of query distribution. <br/>However, each adaptation requires disk I/O, creating overhead that must be amortized across repeated false positives.<br/>Consequently, while adaptive filters deliver robust performance on highly skewed or adversarial workloads, they experience performance degradation on uniform random workloads where adaptivity becomes an unnecessary overhead.<br/><br/>In the talk, we will present ongoing research to address this fundamental limitation in adaptive filters by establishing a connection to the online ski rental problem~\cite{KarlinMMO94}---a classic online decision problem where one must repeatedly choose between renting or buying skis without knowing future usage. <br/>We demonstrate that adapting a false positive is equivalent to ``buying", while accepting the false positive without adaptation corresponds to ``renting''<br/>By analyzing this model, we determine when to adapt a false positive for consistent performance. <br/><br/>Based on this model, we will present details about our proposed solutions: the SkiQF, an online adaptive filter that provides robust false positive rate guarantees through adaptivity while maintaining consistent, high performance across varying query distributions.<br/>We also introduce the SampledSkiQF, a heuristic variant that achieves even stronger performance by trading off slight robustness.<br/>The SampledSkiQF employs a sampling phase to determine an optimal adaptation strategy, deciding between adapting immediately like adaptive filters, or never adapting like traditional filters.<br/><br/>
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>
          </td>
      </tr>


      <tr>
        <td>12:00 - 12:15</td>
        <td>
                    <i>Diva: Dynamic Range Filter for Var-Length Keys and Queries</i><br />
                    Navid Eslami (University of Toronto ); 
Ioana Bercea (KTH Royal Institute of Technology); 
Niv Dayan (University of Toronto)*
<br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po39')">Click to Toggle Abstract</a> 
          <div id="po39" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
We propose to present our VLDB 2025 paper, ``Diva: Dynamic Range Filter for Variable-Length Keys and Queries.'' The paper received the 2025 VLDB Best Paper Award and was recently invited for the 2026 SIGMOD Record Research Highlights. The talk will be given by Niv Dayan, who was a postdoctoral researcher at Harvard University from 2015 to 2019, and is therefore an alumnus of the Northeastern Database Community. Niv is currently an assistant professor at the University of Toronto. <br/><br/>Range filters are compact probabilistic data structures that answer approximate range emptiness queries. They are used in many domains, e.g., in key-value stores, to quickly rule out the existence of keys in a given query range and avoid having to search for them in storage. However, all existing range filters exhibit at least one of the following shortcomings: (1)~ they do not provide robust false positive rate and performance guarantees, (2)~they do not support variable-length keys and query ranges, and (3)~they do not allow dynamic operations such as insertions, deletions, or expansions.<br/><br/>We introduce Diva, the first range filter to address all the above challenges simultaneously. Diva learns the dataset's distribution by sampling keys and storing them in a cache-efficient trie. It compresses the keys in-between samples by removing their longest common prefix and truncating their suffixes while leaving enough bits in the middle (i.e., an infix) to allow differentiating between the keys in the sorted order. It stores infixes in constant time dynamic data blocks, which it splits to handle insertions and expansions. It processes a range query by traversing the trie and checking for the inclusion of infixes in the target query range. We show, theoretically and empirically, that Diva achieves a false positive rate on par with the state of the art on real-world datasets while supporting dynamicity and variable-length queries and keys.
            </div>
        </td>
      </tr>
          </td>
      </tr>

