      <tr>
        <td>2:00 - 2:15</td>
        <td>
                    <i>Understanding Human Judgment in Table Unionability</i><br />
                    Nina Klimenkova (Worcester Polytechnic Institute)*; 
Roee Shraga (Worcester Polytechnic Institute); 
Sreeram Marimuthu (Worcester Polytechnic Institute)
<br />
          <a href="javascript: toggleVisibility ('#po36')">Click to Toggle Abstract</a> 
          <div id="po36" class="abstract" style="display: none;">
            <p>
In this talk, Nina Klimenkova, one of the first authors, will present a study that examines how humans reason about table unionability through a controlled experimental survey and behavioral analysis. The findings are used to develop a machine learning framework that improves raw human judgments and to study how large language models (LLMs) behave under different levels of human context. This talk is based on the ACM SIGMOD 2025 HILDA workshop paper Humans, Machine Learning, and Language Models in Union: A Cognitive Study on Table Unionability.
            </div>
        </td>
